cite6†Leyang Yang , cite7†Xiaoxuan Tang , cite8†Sheng Zhou , cite9†Dajun Chen , cite10†Wei Jiang , cite11†Yong Li L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled History-Aware Reasoning for GUI Agents, by Ziwei Wang and Leyang Yang and Xiaoxuan Tang and Sheng Zhou and Dajun Chen and Wei Jiang and Yong Li\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Advances in Multimodal Large Language Models have significantly enhanced Graphical User Interface (GUI) automation. Equipping GUI agents with reliable episodic reasoning capabilities is essential for bridging the gap between users' concise task descriptions and the complexities of real-world execution. Current methods integrate Reinforcement Learning (RL) with System-2 Chain-of-Thought, yielding notable gains in reasoning enhancement.\nL17: For long-horizon GUI tasks, historical interactions connect each screen to the goal-oriented episode chain, and effectively leveraging these clues is crucial for the current decision. However, existing native GUI agents exhibit weak short-term memory in their explicit reasoning, interpreting the chained interactions as discrete screen understanding, i.e., unawareness of the historical interactions within the episode. This history-agnostic reasoning challenges their performance in GUI automation.\nL18: To alleviate this weakness, we propose a History-Aware Reasoning (HAR) framework, which encourages an agent to reflect on its own errors and acquire episodic reasoning knowledge from them via tailored strategies that enhance short-term memory in long-horizon interaction. The framework mainly comprises constructing a reflective learning scenario, synthesizing tailored correction guidelines, and designing a hybrid RL reward function.\nL19: Using the HAR framework, we develop a native end-to-end model, HAR-GUI-3B, which alters the inherent reasoning mode from history-agnostic to history-aware, equipping the GUI agent with stable short-term memory and reliable perception of screen details. Comprehensive evaluations across a range of GUI-related benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness and generalization of our method.\nL20: Comments:  | Paper accepted to AAAI 2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2511.09127 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2511.09127v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09127†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Ziwei Wang [cite17†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 09:06:25 UTC (7,081 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled History-Aware Reasoning for GUI Agents, by Ziwei Wang and Leyang Yang and Xiaoxuan Tang and Sheng Zhou and Dajun Chen and Wei Jiang and Yong Li\nL35: \nL36:   * cite12†View PDF L37:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite18†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite19†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.AI\nL44: \nL45: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L46: \nL47: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite25†cs L52: cite26†cs.CL L53: cite27†cs.CV L54: cite28†cs.HC L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \nL68: loading...\nL69: \nL70: Data provided by:\nL71: \nL72: ### Bookmark\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL75: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Bibliographic